## 2. Big Picture Architecture

GuardianAI utilizes a modern, decoupled three-tier architecture.

```mermaid
graph TD
    %% Client Tier
    Citizen([Citizen]) -->|Upload Photo + Device GPS| Frontend[React Web App]
    Worker([Worker]) -->|Resolution Photo + Live GPS| Frontend
    Authority([Authority]) -->|Monitor Dashboard| Frontend

    %% Backend API Tier
    Frontend -->|REST API Calls| CoreAPI[Node.js / Express Backend]
    
    %% Storage Tier
    CoreAPI -->|1. Stream Image Buffer| CDN[(Cloudinary CDN)]
    CDN -->|Secure URL| CoreAPI
    CoreAPI -->|Read / Write State| DB[(MongoDB)]
    
    %% ML Microservice Tier
    CoreAPI -->|2. HTTP POST Image URL| ML[Python Flask ML Service]
    ML -->|YOLOv8 Class & Confidence| CoreAPI
    ML -->|ORB Visual Match Score| CoreAPI
```

### Component Breakdown
- **Client Application (React/Vite):** The frontend SPA handles the user interface, role-based rendering, and interactive mapping using Leaflet. It captures device GPS coordinates via the Browser Geolocation API when users submit photos.
- **Core API (Node.js/Express):** The central orchestrator. It manages the database CRUD operations, handles business logic, executes GPS distance calculations, manages SLA (Service Level Agreement) background tasks, and acts as a gateway to the ML service.
- **ML Microservice (Python/Flask):** A decoupled service that performs heavy computer vision tasks. It runs a YOLOv8 object detection model and uses OpenCV for feature matching. By isolating this, the Node.js API remains unblocked and highly responsive.
- **Database (MongoDB):** A NoSQL store managing user profiles and alert documents. The alert documents contain a continuous `history` array to provide an immutable audit trail of state changes.
- **CDN (Cloudinary):** Cloud storage for images. Instead of storing large image blobs in MongoDB or local server disks, images are streamed to Cloudinary, and only the secure URLs are saved in the database.

